1
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:03,860
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:05,760
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:06,240
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:07,600
And my name is Catherine.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,520
And today Marco and I get to share with you the thrilling conclusion to the, well, very popular I must say, series.

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:16,320
I'm sorry, I love you.

7
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:16,900
That's right.

8
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:20,760
Today is the finale, the final episode, episode 10.

9
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,220
This is where it all ends.

10
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,000
So what do you think is going to happen?

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,620
If I guess, you're just going to get angry at me because I actually know how it ends.

12
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,760
But let's just say I hope that it ends well for our protagonists.

13
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:39,100
There was a lot of controversy around it because, you know, I wanted to know if people wanted a happy ending, a sad ending, unexpected.

14
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,940
I guess everyone wants a happy ending, right?

15
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:42,620
Absolutely.

16
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:52,120
So we're going to, well, keep our fingers crossed for Steven and Veronica and check out this dialogue one time.

17
00:00:52,120 --> 00:01:07,240
Steven, good to see you, brother.

18
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:07,540
How are you?

19
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:08,540
How was your trip?

20
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:09,580
It was fine.

21
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,240
I've been better, but it's great to be home.

22
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,200
I've missed you all.

23
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:14,040
How's mom?

24
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:15,880
She's great.

25
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:19,340
All she ever does is talk about you, her little boy that went to the United States.

26
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:20,580
You're her pride and joy.

27
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:22,160
You know that, right?

28
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,160
I can't wait to see her.

29
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:25,160
You?

30
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:25,300
What's new with you?

31
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:27,720
Well, Nisha and I are expecting.

32
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,520
You'll have another nephew or niece soon.

33
00:01:31,290 --> 00:01:32,030
That's great.

34
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,640
Wow.

35
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:33,870
Congrats.

36
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:35,750
You two are great together, you know?

37
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:37,390
You have such a beautiful family.

38
00:01:38,030 --> 00:01:39,530
I hope that one day I can have that.

39
00:01:39,910 --> 00:01:40,630
Of course, man.

40
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:41,190
Come on.

41
00:01:41,550 --> 00:01:44,210
I mean, everything was set here for you to marry Shalini.

42
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:46,450
You know, she's still pining after you.

43
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:48,170
I don't think she'll ever get over you.

44
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:49,670
What are you talking about?

45
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:50,750
I hardly knew her.

46
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:52,170
How could she be in love with me?

47
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:54,790
I couldn't go through with it, even though she's a great woman.

48
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:57,250
No, I left my heart in the United States.

49
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:00,520
I just hope Veronica is happy.

50
00:02:00,970 --> 00:02:01,970
Get over it.

51
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:03,050
You're home now.

52
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:05,470
Everyone here thinks so highly of you.

53
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:07,920
There'll be girls throwing themselves at you.

54
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:09,270
You can marry anyone you want.

55
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:11,030
I don't want to marry anyone.

56
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:12,570
I want to marry her.

57
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:13,650
Don't you understand?

58
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:15,810
You are incorrigible.

59
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:28,600
Steven!

60
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,600
My baby!

61
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:30,600
How are you?

62
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:31,600
I miss you so much!

63
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:32,300
Hey, Mom.

64
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:33,560
Great to see you.

65
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:34,600
You look so thin!

66
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,600
Didn't those Americans feed you?

67
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,600
Come, come.

68
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,600
Let's have some Thai.

69
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:38,600
By the way, there is a girl here waiting for you.

70
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:39,600
Hi, Steven.

71
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:48,320
Veronica, how did you get here?

72
00:02:49,230 --> 00:02:50,210
How do you know where I live?

73
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:53,340
I waited for you at the airport, but you never showed.

74
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,280
I also have some little secrets that I haven't told you about, but we can discuss that later.

75
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:01,900
I realized that I was just scared.

76
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:06,000
Scared of how much I love you and of the commitment that marriage requires.

77
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:07,540
I'm here now.

78
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:09,860
Now there's something I want to ask you.

79
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:28,920
Steven, will you marry me?

80
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:31,360
I now declare you husband and wife.

81
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:32,720
You may kiss the bride.

82
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,360
All right, so they got married.

83
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:42,760
You got married.

84
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:43,700
We got married.

85
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:44,700
I got married.

86
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:47,240
Wow, I should have called my mom.

87
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:54,220
Not only did you get married, but you got married Bollywood style with music and everything nice.

88
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:55,600
Those weddings are like four days long.

89
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,600
I hope you enjoyed the finale of this story.

90
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:01,400
Absolutely.

91
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,320
I was glad to see that it was a happy ending.

92
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:10,760
I have to say if it had been a sad ending, I would have been a little bit disappointed because well, Veronica and I...

93
00:04:10,760 --> 00:04:11,040
You get along.

94
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:12,760
We get along.

95
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:13,760
All right.

96
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:19,380
So apart from this happy ending and this happy finale that we had, we did look at a lot of great words and phrases.

97
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:23,880
So why don't we start now with language takeaway?

98
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:24,320
Language takeaway.

99
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:39,280
So the first couple of phrases I want to talk about here have to do with maybe catching up and talking about family or gossip because we're talking to Stephen's Indian family who hasn't seen for a long, long time.

100
00:04:40,040 --> 00:04:44,600
The first thing he's talking to his brother about his brother and his wife.

101
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:48,160
So Gulam and Nisha are expecting, we find out.

102
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:49,600
Right, they're expecting.

103
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:52,940
So if Gulam and Nisha are expecting, what does that mean exactly?

104
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:54,600
They're expecting a letter.

105
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:56,680
Expecting a baby maybe?

106
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:57,440
Aha, yeah.

107
00:04:57,880 --> 00:04:58,880
So here we go.

108
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:03,280
So I'm expecting is a very common English way of saying I'm going to have a baby.

109
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:03,960
I'm pregnant.

110
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:05,320
All right.

111
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:06,440
So don't get this confused.

112
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:10,020
If somebody says, oh, well, I'm expecting, you don't have to say expecting what?

113
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:10,760
Exactly.

114
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:16,840
When it's only expecting, when it's not a thing afterwards, I'm expecting means I'm pregnant or she's expecting.

115
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:18,360
She's going to have a baby.

116
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:21,860
So they are expecting a baby or they're expecting.

117
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:34,880
And they were talking about, well, Stephen, he was supposed to get married in India and his brother said a very interesting, word he said, well, these girls are still, this girl is still pining over you.

118
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:41,130
So the Shalini, some girl that Stephen used to know when he was living in India, she's pining after you.

119
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:45,260
Well, pine has many different meanings in English.

120
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:49,850
But in this sense, we're talking about having a crush on or being in love with.

121
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:55,590
And also it's a way of saying that you are, that you desire, you yearn for this person very, very much.

122
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,200
But that you don't have them.

123
00:05:57,200 --> 00:05:58,200
Right.

124
00:05:58,350 --> 00:05:58,910
You want them.

125
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:00,200
You want them.

126
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,200
So you're pining over this person.

127
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,280
Now, can you pine over an object?

128
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:04,040
No.

129
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:05,280
No.

130
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:08,660
Usually it's maybe related to love or to a person, right?

131
00:06:08,960 --> 00:06:09,140
Yeah.

132
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:14,380
So she, she stands by the door every day waiting the letter that doesn't come.

133
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,700
She's pining away for her dear love, Thomas or whatever.

134
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:22,400
And you know what I read recently is that people can get addicted to pining.

135
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:23,280
Really?

136
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:23,600
Yeah.

137
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:34,960
So maybe you can pine after a person not because you miss them or you want to, but you kind of grow accustomed to it and you become addicted to this pain that's kind of pleasurable.

138
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:36,410
There is a way to describe this.

139
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:38,160
It's called Petrarchan love.

140
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:44,820
Well, it's this kind of traditional idea in poetry where you have a love that's never consummated, that's never filled.

141
00:06:45,240 --> 00:06:48,640
You never get to be with the person that you love, that you desire.

142
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:53,460
And so it's a way, yeah, it's a way of distinguishing the two different kinds of love.

143
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:58,160
Some where you're with the person and the other where you always want them, but you can never have them.

144
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:03,360
Well, hopefully none of our listeners are pining over anyone or, or get to experience that, huh?

145
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:04,400
Exactly.

146
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:13,600
But if you do pine away or you pine after someone, you have to hope that one day you'll get over that person.

147
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:15,200
Yeah, you have to get over it.

148
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:18,040
And that's what his brother says, hey, get over it.

149
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,200
I don't think she'll ever get over you.

150
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:23,300
So what does it mean when you're supposed to get over someone?

151
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:30,280
Okay, this is a good question because, you know, we're talking here not about actually getting up on top of a fence or a wall.

152
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:31,880
We're talking about emotions.

153
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:41,840
So getting over someone, so I have to get over him means that I have to stop feeling so sad about what, what happened, right?

154
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,300
Because usually you say this when someone's sad or angry.

155
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:49,860
And also it kind of means that you have to forget or let that person go, you know, like get over it.

156
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:54,580
We're going to move on here to the last word in today's language takeaway, which is incorrigible.

157
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:57,380
Okay, an incorrigible person.

158
00:07:57,760 --> 00:07:59,920
Alright, incorrigible.

159
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:00,560
What could this mean?

160
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,000
Well, let's look at the sentence where we find it.

161
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:05,160
You are incorrigible.

162
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:07,220
Gulam says this to Stephen.

163
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:08,760
Right.

164
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:12,660
So it's an adjective and basically it means that you have no correction of some sort.

165
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:15,560
Well, there's an argument between Stephen and Gulam.

166
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:16,640
So they're brothers.

167
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:18,080
And Stephen says, I don't want to get married.

168
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:19,060
I don't want to marry her.

169
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:23,280
He's complaining and his brother says, you are incorrigible.

170
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:24,360
You just won't stop.

171
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:31,480
So someone's feelings, their emotions, their ideas about something, they just can't be changed or altered.

172
00:08:31,880 --> 00:08:32,140
They're fixed.

173
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:35,240
You know, this guy, he's like, I'm not going to get married.

174
00:08:35,240 --> 00:08:37,300
And his brother says, you're incorrigible.

175
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:38,720
I just can't change your mind.

176
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:39,740
It's kind of like being stubborn.

177
00:08:40,140 --> 00:08:41,100
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

178
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,860
And we hear this phrase a lot together.

179
00:08:44,260 --> 00:08:45,600
So you're incorrigible.

180
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:53,540
So this is often just kind of a, it's not an insult, but it's a way for us to kind of be sarcastic or to make fun of someone.

181
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:55,380
You're incorrigible, Marco.

182
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:58,280
Usually you say it when you're loud, you know, you're being nice.

183
00:08:59,240 --> 00:08:59,980
Okay, cool.

184
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:03,640
So five great words in language takeaway today.

185
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:08,920
And well, before we move on and listen to the dialogue again, why don't we take a look at Fluency Builder?

186
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:11,150
Fluency Builder.

187
00:09:12,030 --> 00:09:12,390
All right.

188
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:20,760
So this phrase is something that parents often use and grandparents as well, because obviously parents and grandparents, they're very proud of their children, their grandchildren.

189
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:24,550
So they describe something as being their pride and joy.

190
00:09:24,890 --> 00:09:26,090
My pride and joy.

191
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:29,270
So it's usually a person that's your pride and joy.

192
00:09:29,430 --> 00:09:30,170
It could be a thing too.

193
00:09:30,430 --> 00:09:30,970
It could be a thing.

194
00:09:31,330 --> 00:09:33,670
His Porsche Boxster is his pride and joy.

195
00:09:33,850 --> 00:09:35,850
He goes out every morning and he washes it.

196
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:39,720
He changes the oil, he checks the gas, checks the tires.

197
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,220
So it's his pride and joy.

198
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:42,520
All right.

199
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:43,880
It's his most valued possession.

200
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:45,700
So it always goes together, right?

201
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:46,640
My pride and joy.

202
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:47,460
Pride and joy.

203
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:49,260
Yeah.

204
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:50,260
Just think of it as a phrase, pride and joy.

205
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:51,260
All right.

206
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:53,920
So I'm my mother's pride and joy.

207
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:54,440
I think you are.

208
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:56,380
All right.

209
00:09:56,660 --> 00:09:57,680
So you don't know my mom.

210
00:09:58,980 --> 00:09:59,640
All right.

211
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,020
So somebody's pride and joy.

212
00:10:02,410 --> 00:10:09,110
So now another phrase that we took a look at, and it's a way of making conversation, especially with a person that you haven't seen in a while.

213
00:10:09,210 --> 00:10:11,010
He said, Hey, what's new with you?

214
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:12,880
What's new with you?

215
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:14,500
What's new with you?

216
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:15,000
All right.

217
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,520
So this might sound a little strange because normally we say, how have you been?

218
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:19,440
It's been a long time.

219
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:20,020
How are you?

220
00:10:20,220 --> 00:10:22,680
But this is like, you know, it's a fluency builder phrase.

221
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:26,240
So it's very colloquial, it's very spoken language.

222
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,660
This you hear often between friends or family members.

223
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:30,960
Hey, Marco, what's new with you?

224
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:33,840
So how do you, I would answer this by saying, oh, well, not much.

225
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:36,100
Or I just bought a new house.

226
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:39,480
So basically you're asking for news from a person.

227
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:40,520
I haven't seen you in a long time.

228
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:42,400
You look, you look happy and healthy.

229
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:43,600
So what's new with you?

230
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:44,380
Oh, I got married.

231
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:45,720
I got a new car.

232
00:10:47,040 --> 00:10:49,160
These things that you can say, these are changes in my life.

233
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:49,660
All right.

234
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:53,160
And usually after somebody asks you, what's new with you?

235
00:10:53,480 --> 00:10:56,720
You say something and then you usually would follow up and ask that person as well, right?

236
00:10:57,120 --> 00:10:58,220
What's new and what's new with you?

237
00:10:58,600 --> 00:10:58,720
Exactly.

238
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,300
So, you know, I got a new house, got a new job.

239
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:02,460
What's new with you?

240
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Yeah.

241
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Or what about you?

242
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:06,000
What about you?

243
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,600
So it's a nice, very relaxed, informal way of asking someone for news in their life.

244
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:10,860
Perfect.

245
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:12,600
All right.

246
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:13,600
What's new with you?

247
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:14,600
What else?

248
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:15,600
Now, he was supposed to get married, right?

249
00:11:15,600 --> 00:11:16,600
Right.

250
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:17,600
So this is news.

251
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:18,600
He was supposed to get married.

252
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:19,600
He didn't get married.

253
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:20,600
What's going on?

254
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:22,600
His brother, his mother, they all want to know what's going on.

255
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:27,720
And we find out that wedding, that girl, he just couldn't go through with it.

256
00:11:28,360 --> 00:11:30,460
So I can't, I couldn't go through with it.

257
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:31,000
Okay.

258
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:32,680
This past tense.

259
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:36,160
So he's saying, before I came to India with this girl, I couldn't go through with it.

260
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:38,160
So present would be, I can't go through with it.

261
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:41,040
And going through with what here?

262
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:44,960
We've got it, this kind of magical, strange word, it.

263
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:45,620
What could this be?

264
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:51,440
So basically, what he's saying is that he couldn't complete or maybe finish something that he started.

265
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:52,320
Right.

266
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:55,440
So in this case, we're talking about a relationship.

267
00:11:55,440 --> 00:11:55,660
Or marriage.

268
00:11:56,440 --> 00:11:56,780
Yeah, a relationship.

269
00:11:57,440 --> 00:12:00,500
So maybe we loved each other, but I couldn't go through with it.

270
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:02,300
I couldn't go to the finish line.

271
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:03,260
Right.

272
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:04,180
I couldn't get married.

273
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:08,140
You see that a lot when maybe in movies, two people are supposed to get married.

274
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:13,680
They're at the altar, and all of a sudden the bride says, I can't, or she goes away crying.

275
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:15,720
I can't go through with this.

276
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:17,560
Exactly.

277
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:20,690
So because she loves somebody else, so can't go through with it.

278
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:21,250
Great.

279
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:22,450
So really good phrase.

280
00:12:23,070 --> 00:12:24,370
I can't go through with it.

281
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:26,070
He couldn't go through with it.

282
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,800
And we find out that, well, not such a bad thing.

283
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:31,680
Steven, he's very popular in India.

284
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:32,760
The girls love him.

285
00:12:32,940 --> 00:12:37,360
We've got a great phrase for describing having lots of girls who love you.

286
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:38,320
Right.

287
00:12:38,440 --> 00:12:41,520
He says there will be girls throwing themselves at you.

288
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:42,320
Literally?

289
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:43,960
Like, ow, that might hurt.

290
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:45,080
I can't imagine that.

291
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:47,640
Like, just somebody throwing themselves at you?

292
00:12:47,640 --> 00:12:48,740
No, it's not literal.

293
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:51,920
It's basically saying girls are very interested in you.

294
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:55,100
Girls are so interested in you that they'll be chasing you almost.

295
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:55,660
All right.

296
00:12:55,660 --> 00:12:58,300
So many, many girls, they will follow you.

297
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:00,880
They'll want to be in a relationship with you.

298
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:01,880
They like you.

299
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:04,140
So he's saying, listen, there are other girls.

300
00:13:04,840 --> 00:13:04,960
Don't worry.

301
00:13:05,840 --> 00:13:07,600
Girls here, they love you.

302
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:08,860
And it doesn't only work with girls, right?

303
00:13:08,980 --> 00:13:11,640
I can say there'll be boys throwing themselves at you.

304
00:13:11,760 --> 00:13:11,900
Right.

305
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:16,060
So this is something we say about both genders, boys and girls.

306
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:19,360
You could say, oh, God, all the boys were throwing themselves at her.

307
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:20,260
I'm so jealous.

308
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:21,740
Right.

309
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:25,140
But it's only basically in a loving way, right?

310
00:13:25,300 --> 00:13:26,440
It can't be anything else.

311
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:28,200
Like somebody's chasing you for other reasons.

312
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,360
No, this is saying that they have crushes, they have feelings, they want to be romantic.

313
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:38,630
It's not a bad thing, but sometimes it's too much attention, so it can be kind of negative.

314
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:39,960
Yeah.

315
00:13:39,960 --> 00:13:40,960
All right.

316
00:13:40,960 --> 00:13:42,330
Well, interesting phrases on Fluency Builder today.

317
00:13:42,590 --> 00:13:46,010
I think it's time for us to listen to this dialogue for one last time.

318
